'''vacant''' (vā′kant), ''a.'' [Early mod. E. also ''vacaunt''; < ME. ''vacaunt'', < OF. (and F.) ''vacant'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''vacante'', < L. ''vacan(t-)s'', empty, vacant, ppr. of ''vacare'', be empty, free, or unoccupied: see ''vacate''.] '''1.''' Having no contents: empty; unfilled; void; devoid; destitute: as, a ''vacant'' space; a ''vacant'' room.

{{smaller block|Being of those virtues ''vacant''.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. VIII., v. 1. 126.}}}}

